<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01JA">Example <span data-tag="01JA">4.6.1.7</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a simplicial set containing vertices $X$ and $Y$. Let $K$ be a simplicial set, and let $\underline{X}, \underline{Y}: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the constant maps taking the values $X$ and $Y$, respectively. Then there is a canonical isomorphism of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \underline{X}, \underline{Y} ) \simeq \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) ). \]
</div>
</article>
